selessly informs them of  his works which means that it informs  them with something like an intellectual  communication all right and we can put  that aside for the moment because then  he immediately goes to this conundrum  that is being raised well if God created  the heavens and earth and and that means  that God created the entire world and  indeed the entire universe the cosmos  why did he do it then why did he do it  right then why did God choose that point  in time to create things why didn't he  do it earlier why didn't you do it later  what Augustine is probing here is the  intelligibility of the divine creation  of everything else that is not God he's  saying what's the  and this is indeed a question that  people ask he considers this in the  confessions as well where he makes a  joke about it which you can find in  there but here he considers a few  solutions that in his view aren't really  solution so one one solution is to say  well God didn't create the heavens and  earth all right the world is eternal and  uncreated it's always existed you can  have God if you like it but you know God  didn't actually create the world so  that's that's you know one approach that  you can take and that would answer the  question but Augustine says that one's  not really going to work he says those  who wish to make out that the world is  eternal and without beginning was not  made by God are deceived and Raven the  incurable madness of impiety and he says  how do we know this well we can look at  the world itself and see that it had to  be made by something and it wasn't  auto-generating you could say and this  isn't a very strong argument but it's  the argument that he makes there then he  goes on and he raises another issue and  he's got an another strange sort of  solution to this he says well you could  have a world that is caused by God but  was never created at any moment in time  it never it never came to be it's it has  a creational but not temporal beginning 