ve outside the world  infinite realms of space which if anyone  says the omnipotent cannot hold his hand  from working will it not follow they  must adopt you Epicurus his dream of  innumerable worlds with this difference  only he asserts they're formed and  destroyed by the fortuitous movements of  atoms and they'll say they're formed by  God's hands so why did God create this  world at this point in time in this spot  Augustine says that's a mistaken way of  looking at it God didn't create it in  this spot  this spot is what he created God didn't  create it at this point in time when  creation occurred that's when time began  so it does have a temporal beginning  creation but it doesn't have a temporal  you could say precedence it  there's no time before it and why is  this the case  well Augustine is saying if we think  about the nature of time what is time  you know we often picture to ourselves  this line that we use with past present  future and we have different ways of  depicting it to ourselves like maybe  we're facing into the future and moving  into it you notice we have to use  spatial metaphors in order to understand  time or we're looking back in memory at  the past and the past doesn't really  exist neither does the future and the  present is just this tiny little sliver  but we depict it through ourselves is  this thing moving forward into the  future all these little moments  comprised right again we spatialize time  as many philosophers have pointed out to  us but how are we doing that how are we  depicting time to ourselves what we  require in order to do that is some sort  of change or movement  we cannot conceptualize and to pick time  without depending on something changing  so you know think about your your watch  if you have one you know think about the  old traditional one with the hands you  watch the hand going around that's  movement or if it's a water clock you  watch the little droplets of water or an  hourglass right we measure time in